package com.hpcai270

// Days 254–270: senior engineering, architecture, operations, leadership and interview mastery.
val hpcDaysPart21 = listOf(
Day(254,"Reference architecture thinking","Requirements before products","Senior architects start with requirements: workload, scale, performance, availability, security, lifecycle, operations and cost.","QUESTIONS\n\nGPU count? Dataset size? Read/write profile? Metadata rate? Checkpoints? RPO/RTO? Growth? Users? Fabric? Existing standards? Budget? Skills?",
"OUTPUT\n\nRequirements → constraints → architecture options → trade-offs → selected design → validation plan.","LAB\n\nCreate a requirements document for a 1,000-GPU AI platform before naming any vendor.","INTERVIEW\n\nIf the interviewer asks ‘Which storage would you choose?’, ask at least five clarifying questions before answering.","GOAL\n\nDemonstrate architecture maturity by delaying premature product selection."),
Day(255,"AI factory reference architecture","Compute, network, storage and software as one platform","A production AI factory combines GPU compute, high-speed fabrics, storage tiers, management, orchestration and software. NVIDIA reference architectures explicitly integrate these layers and certified storage partners.","FLOW\n\nUSERS → CONTROL/SLURM/K8S → GPU COMPUTE → COMPUTE FABRIC\n                         ↘ STORAGE FABRIC → HPS/PARALLEL FS\n                         ↘ USER STORAGE/NFS → OBJECT/ARCHIVE\n                         ↘ MONITORING/SECURITY",
"DESIGN\n\nKeep management and data paths clear. Size storage and network together. Provide independent recovery paths where required.","LAB\n\nDraw a 512-GPU reference architecture with two storage tiers, InfiniBand/RoCE fabric, management Ethernet and object archive.","INTERVIEW\n\nExplain why high-performance storage and user storage may have different optimization goals: bulk parallel I/O versus metadata/IOPS and operational access.","GOAL\n\nThink in full-platform reference architectures."),
Day(256,"Availability engineering","SLOs, redundancy and failure budgets","Availability is an engineered property. Redundancy without tested failover is not proof of availability.","MODEL\n\nSLO → failure modes → redundancy → detection → failover → recovery → validation.","STORAGE\n\nRedundant nodes, paths, targets and metadata services must align with actual failure domains.","LAB\n\nBuild a failure-mode table for a storage platform: disk, node, NIC, switch, rack, site, software corruption.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhat is the difference between HA and DR? HA handles expected component failures with service continuity; DR handles larger recovery scenarios such as site loss.","GOAL\n\nDesign availability from failure scenarios."),
Day(257,"Chaos and failure testing","Prove resilience instead of assuming it","Controlled failure testing validates that the architecture behaves as designed.","TESTS\n\nDisconnect one path, drain one node, stop a service, fill a test filesystem, simulate target failure, isolate a network link.","RULES\n\nDefine blast radius, rollback, observers, success criteria and maintenance window. Never experiment destructively on uncontrolled production.","AI\n\nMeasure training impact during failure: checkpoint continuity, job behavior, GPU utilization and recovery time.","LAB\n\nCreate a quarterly resilience test calendar for a GPU/storage platform.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy is failure testing an engineering activity rather than a support activity? It validates architectural assumptions.","GOAL\n\nMake resilience measurable."),
Day(258,"Incident command","Lead a major storage outage","Senior engineers coordinate people as well as commands. Incident response needs roles, communication, evidence preservation and decision discipline.","FLOW\n\nDeclare → scope → stabilize → investigate → mitigate → recover → validate → communicate → postmortem.","COMMUNICATION\n\nState facts, uncertainty and next update. Avoid speculation presented as root cause.","LAB\n\nWrite a major-incident timeline for an AI storage outage affecting 512 GPUs. Include technical and stakeholder updates.","INTERVIEW\n\nTell a story using Situation → impact → evidence → action → result → prevention. Focus on engineering judgment.","GOAL\n\nShow leadership without needing a management title."),
Day(259,"Root cause analysis","Find the systemic cause","RCA should explain why the system allowed the incident and why detection/prevention did not stop it.","FIVE WHYS\n\nUse causal reasoning, but avoid stopping at ‘human error’. Ask why the process/system made the error likely.","AI EXAMPLE\n\nCheckpoint failure → filesystem full → growth alert missed → alert not routed → ownership unclear → monitoring design gap.","LAB\n\nWrite an RCA with contributing factors, evidence, corrective actions and preventive controls.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhat makes a good postmortem? Blameless facts, causal chain, impact, detection gap and concrete actions with owners.","GOAL\n\nImprove systems, not just close tickets."),
Day(260,"Capacity forecasting","Predict the future before it becomes an outage","Capacity management combines historical growth, planned workloads and performance trends.","MODEL\n\nCurrent use + growth rate + project pipeline + protection overhead + operational reserve → forecast date.","AI\n\nGPU purchases can create sudden storage demand. Capacity plans should be synchronized with compute expansion.","LAB\n\nBuild a 12-month forecast for capacity, bandwidth and metadata load. Identify the month when expansion must begin.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy should storage planning start before GPU procurement? Because storage and fabric lead time can constrain the new cluster.","GOAL\n\nBecome proactive rather than reactive."),
Day(261,"Technology evaluation","Proof of concept that proves something","A PoC should answer a decision question. Avoid demos that only prove a product can run.","POC DESIGN\n\nHypothesis → workload → baseline → success criteria → test plan → evidence → decision.","AI\n\nTest realistic dataset, concurrency, checkpointing, failure and scaling. Include operational effort, not just peak bandwidth.","LAB\n\nWrite a PoC plan comparing two storage platforms for a 128-GPU workload.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhat would make you reject a high-performing product? Poor reliability, operational complexity, weak ecosystem, security gap or inability to meet SLOs.","GOAL\n\nEvaluate technology scientifically."),
Day(262,"Vendor architecture review","Ask vendors senior-level questions","Storage vendors will present architectures and benchmarks. Your job is to test assumptions and understand what is actually included.","QUESTIONS\n\nWhat workload produced the number? How many clients? What protection? What network? What metadata rate? What recovery performance? What is the failure behavior?",
"CONTRACT\n\nClarify support boundaries, firmware/software lifecycle, upgrade path, spares, response time and compatibility.","LAB\n\nCreate a vendor questionnaire for a 1 PB AI storage purchase.","INTERVIEW\n\nHow do you challenge a vendor without being adversarial? Ask precise technical questions and request reproducible evidence.","GOAL\n\nOperate at customer/architect level."),
Day(263,"Documentation engineering","Architecture is incomplete until it can be operated","Production knowledge must be transferable. Good documents show architecture, assumptions, dependencies, procedures, validation and failure handling.","DOCUMENT SET\n\nHLD → LLD → rack/cabling → IP plan → storage layout → runbooks → monitoring → DR → upgrade plan → troubleshooting matrix.","AI\n\nInclude GPU/NIC topology and data paths, not just storage configuration.","LAB\n\nWrite a 5-page HLD for a 256-GPU platform. Add a one-page incident runbook.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy document assumptions? Because architecture decisions become invalid when workload or hardware changes.","GOAL\n\nMake yourself replaceable and the platform operable."),
Day(264,"Change management","Safe change in high-performance infrastructure","AI clusters are tightly coupled. Firmware, drivers, filesystem clients and fabric configuration can have compatibility dependencies.","CHANGE FLOW\n\nRisk → dependency review → test → canary → maintenance → validation → rollout → rollback readiness.","LAB\n\nPlan a NIC firmware upgrade across 256 nodes. Include canary nodes, drain, monitoring and rollback criteria.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhat makes a change high risk? Large blast radius, uncertain compatibility, difficult rollback or business-critical workload impact.","GOAL\n\nChange deliberately and measurably."),
Day(265,"Security for senior storage engineers","Threat modeling the data plane","Senior storage engineers should recognize threats even when security teams own policy.","THREATS\n\nCredential theft, unauthorized dataset access, malicious writes, ransomware, exposed management APIs, supply-chain compromise and lateral movement.","CONTROLS\n\nLeast privilege, segmentation, MFA for management, secrets management, signed artifacts, immutable backups, audit and vulnerability management.","LAB\n\nThreat-model a storage management API and identify five attack paths plus mitigations.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhat is the most dangerous storage credential? The one with broad administrative rights and long-lived access. Explain how to reduce blast radius.","GOAL\n\nInclude security in architecture and operations."),
Day(266,"Senior troubleshooting drill","Cross-layer diagnosis","Now combine Linux, storage, network, scheduler and GPU evidence.","SCENARIO\n\nTraining throughput drops 50%. GPU utilization falls. Storage p99 latency rises. Network shows increased ECN marks. A storage target has normal media latency.","REASONING\n\nThe evidence points away from media and toward fabric congestion or queueing. Verify client/network path, per-port counters, workload distribution and topology before changing storage.","LAB\n\nWrite a hypothesis tree and list the single measurement that would most efficiently distinguish each branch.","INTERVIEW\n\nExplain your reasoning aloud in under five minutes. Senior candidates communicate the decision path, not just commands.","GOAL\n\nDemonstrate cross-domain troubleshooting."),
Day(267,"Senior architecture interview","Design an AI platform from scratch","You are asked to design storage and networking for 1,024 GPUs, 20 PB datasets and multi-tenant training.","REQUIREMENTS\n\nStart with bandwidth, metadata, checkpoint, concurrency, RPO/RTO, growth, tenancy, security and budget questions.","REFERENCE\n\nGPU nodes → high-speed fabric → parallel filesystem/HPS → object/archive; separate user/management access; scheduler and monitoring integrated.","TRADE-OFFS\n\nLustre vs Storage Scale vs modern data platform; IB vs RoCE; flash vs capacity; local NVMe cache; protection and recovery.","LAB\n\nProduce a complete HLD and a sizing worksheet. State every assumption.","INTERVIEW\n\nExpect follow-ups: ‘What fails first?’, ‘How do you prove 500 GB/s?’, ‘What happens during rack loss?’, ‘How do you upgrade it?’",
"GOAL\n\nAnswer architecture questions with a repeatable framework."),
Day(268,"Senior storage interview","Technical + behavioral mastery","The final interview is not only commands. It tests depth, judgment, communication and ownership.","TECHNICAL\n\nLinux → storage protocols → NVMe/NVMe-oF → Lustre/Scale/Ceph → RDMA/IB/RoCE → performance → AI data path → cloud.","SCENARIOS\n\nLow GPU utilization, checkpoint slow, metadata storm, target failure, network congestion, capacity emergency, upgrade failure.","BEHAVIORAL\n\nDifficult incident, disagreement with vendor, risky change, automation failure, mentoring, prioritization and customer communication.","LAB\n\nRecord yourself answering 25 questions. Remove jargon that you cannot explain and strengthen evidence-based examples.","GOAL\n\nSound like a senior engineer who can own a production platform."),
Day(269,"Final practical examination","Prove the whole stack","This is a four-part exam: architecture, performance, troubleshooting and operations.","PART 1 — ARCHITECTURE\n\nDesign a 512-GPU platform and defend every layer.","PART 2 — PERFORMANCE\n\nDesign FIO/IOR/application tests and explain the scaling curve.","PART 3 — TROUBLESHOOTING\n\nDiagnose low GPU utilization using storage/network/CPU evidence.","PART 4 — OPERATIONS\n\nWrite a change plan, failure runbook, monitoring dashboard and DR test.","INTERVIEW\n\nYou pass when you can explain not only what you would do, but why, what evidence proves it and what risk remains.","GOAL\n\nDemonstrate engineering judgment under uncertainty."),
Day(270,"HPC + AI Storage Architect","The complete professional standard","Day 270 is the final field manual standard. You should now think like a senior infrastructure engineer moving into HPC/AI storage architecture, not like a beginner collecting certifications.","MASTER MENTAL MODEL\n\nUSER → SCHEDULER/ORCHESTRATOR → APPLICATION → CPU/GPU MEMORY → PCIe/NIC → FABRIC → PARALLEL STORAGE/DATA PLATFORM → FLASH/CAPACITY → OBJECT/ARCHIVE.\n\nAround it: identity, security, observability, automation, capacity, lifecycle and recovery.","WHAT YOU SHOULD KNOW\n\nLinux administration; storage protocols; NFS/S3; NVMe/NVMe-oF; RAID/protection; Lustre; Storage Scale; Ceph; RDMA; InfiniBand; RoCE; congestion/QoS; FIO/IOR; Slurm; Kubernetes; GDS; NCCL concepts; AI data pipelines; cloud/hybrid; Terraform; Python/Bash/Ansible; production operations.","WHAT 12 YEARS OF INDUSTRY EXPERIENCE ADDS\n\nIncident discipline, change management, customer communication, vendor management, capacity planning, documentation, risk awareness and understanding that production is more than a benchmark. Your existing storage career should be treated as the operational foundation of this specialization.","WHAT 2 YEARS OF HPC/AI EXPERIENCE SHOULD LOOK LIKE\n\nYou should be able to deploy or operate a lab cluster, explain real production architectures, run and interpret benchmarks, troubleshoot across storage/network/compute, participate in architecture reviews and communicate trade-offs to customers and engineering teams. Training alone does not create production years; hands-on evidence and real incidents do.","FINAL CAPSTONE\n\nBuild a documented 256–512 GPU reference platform in your lab or a realistic simulator. Produce HLD, LLD, topology, storage layout, benchmark plan, monitoring dashboard, failure runbooks, DR plan, automation and a 30-minute architecture presentation. Keep the work in a portfolio repository.","FINAL INTERVIEW QUESTION\n\n‘Why should we hire you for an HPC/AI storage role?’\n\nAnswer honestly: you bring deep enterprise storage operations and are deliberately extending that foundation into distributed filesystems, high-speed fabrics, performance engineering and AI data infrastructure. Then prove it with diagrams, labs, measurements and incident reasoning.","STANDARD\n\nThe objective is not to memorize 270 days. The objective is to build a durable mental model: data must move from source to GPU reliably, fast enough, securely and economically; every layer can fail; every performance claim must be measured; and every architecture must be operable.")
)
